PART 4: CHANGE STATE WITHOUT FULL PAGE RELOAD

================================================================================

Core Idea

A React application can update its UI when state changes without requiring a full browser page reload.

Traditional Page-Based Flow
USER ACTION
     ↓
REQUEST TO SERVER
     ↓
SERVER PROCESSES REQUEST
     ↓
SERVER RETURNS PAGE
     ↓
BROWSER LOADS PAGE
     ↓
USER SEES UPDATED CONTENT

React State-Driven Flow
USER ACTION
     ↓
EVENT HANDLER
     ↓
STATE UPDATE
     ↓
COMPONENT RE-RENDERS
     ↓
REACT COMMITS REQUIRED UI CHANGES
     ↓
BROWSER DISPLAYS UPDATE

Main Difference
Traditional Page Approach     | React State Update
------------------------------|--------------------------------
Page navigation may be needed | No full reload for local state change
Server may return HTML        | UI derives from component state
Whole page may reload         | Required UI changes are committed
Page-oriented                 | Interactive/state-driven


================================================================================

CODE EXAMPLE: STATE CHANGE WITHOUT RELOAD

================================================================================

import React, { useState } from 'react';

export default function Counter() {

  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  return (
    <div>

      <h1>
        Count: {count}
      </h1>

      <button
        onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}
      >
        Increment
      </button>

    </div>
  );
}

Code Explanation
Code / Concept                      | Explanation
------------------------------------|--------------------------------------
useState(0)                         | Creates state with initial value 0
count                               | Current state value
setCount                            | Function used to update count
Count: {count}                      | Displays current state
onClick                             | Handles button click
setCount(count + 1)                 | Updates count state

User Experience
Step | Action
-----|------------------------------------------------
1    | Component initially displays Count: 0
2    | User clicks Increment
3    | setCount(count + 1) executes
4    | Count changes from 0 to 1
5    | Component re-renders
6    | UI displays Count: 1
7    | No full browser page reload is required


Easy flow:

CLICK
  ↓
setCount()
  ↓
STATE CHANGES
  ↓
RE-RENDER
  ↓
UI UPDATES


