PART 5: REACT'S DECLARATIVE APPROACH

================================================================================

What is Declarative Programming?

Declarative programming means describing what the UI should look like for the current data/state, rather than manually describing every DOM operation.

Example:

<h1>Hello {name}</h1>


You describe:

"I want an h1 containing Hello + current name"


React handles the rendering process needed to reflect the current value.

================================================================================

DECLARATIVE VS IMPERATIVE

================================================================================

Imperative Approach - Vanilla JavaScript
const heading =
  document.getElementById('heading');

heading.textContent =
  'Hello ' + name;


You explicitly:

Step | Action
-----|-------------------------
1    | Find the element
2    | Reference/select it
3    | Create the text
4    | Update the DOM

Declarative Approach - React
<h1>Hello {name}</h1>


You describe the desired output based on name.

Comparison
Imperative                     | Declarative
-------------------------------|----------------------------------
Describe HOW to update UI      | Describe WHAT UI should be
Explicit DOM operations        | UI expressed from data/state
Developer coordinates updates  | React manages rendering process
More step-by-step instructions | Focus on desired result


================================================================================

WHY DECLARATIVE PROGRAMMING IS USEFUL

================================================================================

It can provide:

Clearer intent.
Less routine DOM manipulation.
Easier state-to-UI reasoning.
Better maintainability as UI complexity increases.
Example: Todo List
Imperative - Vanilla JavaScript
const list =
  document.getElementById('todoList');

list.innerHTML = '';

todos.forEach(todo => {

  const li =
    document.createElement('li');

  li.textContent =
    todo.text;

  list.appendChild(li);
});

Declarative - React
{todos.map(todo => (
  <li key={todo.id}>
    {todo.text}
  </li>
))}

Easy Comparison
Vanilla JavaScript

Find List
   ↓
Clear List
   ↓
Create Elements
   ↓
Set Content
   ↓
Append Elements


React

TODOS DATA
   ↓
map()
   ↓
DESCRIBE <li> ELEMENTS
   ↓
REACT RENDERS UI


