PART 7: HOW REACT WORKS - THE FLOW

================================================================================

Step 1: Initial Render

A simplified flow is:

REACT COMPONENT
      ↓
JSX
      ↓
JAVASCRIPT REPRESENTATION
      ↓
REACT RENDERING
      ↓
DOM COMMIT
      ↓
BROWSER DISPLAYS UI


JSX itself is syntax that is transformed into JavaScript before/during the build process rather than being HTML directly.

Step 2: State Changes
USER INTERACTION
(click, type, etc.)
      ↓
EVENT HANDLER
      ↓
STATE UPDATE
      ↓
COMPONENT RE-RENDERS
      ↓
REACT RECONCILES OUTPUT
      ↓
REQUIRED DOM CHANGES COMMITTED
      ↓
BROWSER DISPLAYS UPDATED UI


================================================================================

CODE EXAMPLE: REACT FLOW

================================================================================

import React, { useState } from 'react';

export default function App() {

  // Step 1: Initial state
  const [message, setMessage] =
    useState('Hello');

  // Step 2: Event handler
  const handleClick = () => {

    // Step 3: Update state
    setMessage('Button was clicked!');
  };

  // Step 4: Return JSX
  return (
    <div>

      <h1>
        {message}
      </h1>

      <button onClick={handleClick}>
        Click Me
      </button>

    </div>
  );
}

What Happens?
Step | What Happens
-----|---------------------------------------------------
1    | message state starts as "Hello"
2    | Component renders
3    | User sees "Hello" and the button
4    | User clicks button
5    | handleClick() executes
6    | setMessage() updates the state
7    | Component re-renders
8    | message becomes "Button was clicked!"
9    | Updated message appears
10   | No full page reload is required

Flow
message = "Hello"
      ↓
CLICK
      ↓
handleClick()
      ↓
setMessage("Button was clicked!")
      ↓
STATE UPDATE
      ↓
RE-RENDER
      ↓
UPDATED UI


