PART 8: VIRTUAL DOM AND RECONCILIATION

================================================================================

What is the Virtual DOM?

The term Virtual DOM commonly refers to an in-memory representation of UI elements used by React as part of its rendering/reconciliation process.

Characteristic        | Description
----------------------|---------------------------------------------
Location              | In memory
Representation        | JavaScript-based description of UI
Purpose               | Support React's rendering process
Reconciliation        | Determine how new output differs
Final Result          | Required updates committed to DOM

Simplified Flow
1. Component renders
        ↓
2. React creates new render output
        ↓
3. State changes
        ↓
4. Component renders again
        ↓
5. React reconciles previous and new output
        ↓
6. React determines required changes
        ↓
7. Appropriate DOM updates are committed
        ↓
8. Browser displays updated UI


================================================================================

WHY THIS APPROACH IS USEFUL

================================================================================

Direct browser DOM operations can involve work such as:

DOM CHANGE
   ↓
STYLE CALCULATION
   ↓
POSSIBLE LAYOUT/REFLOW
   ↓
POSSIBLE PAINT
   ↓
UPDATED SCREEN


Not every DOM change causes all these stages, but unnecessary DOM work can hurt performance.

React allows developers to describe the desired UI and lets React coordinate the required DOM updates.

Comparison
Direct DOM Example
list.innerHTML = '';

todos.forEach(todo => {
  // Create/update DOM manually
});

React
{todos.map(todo => (
  <li key={todo.id}>
    {todo.text}
  </li>
))}

Direct DOM Approach            | React Approach
-------------------------------|-----------------------------------
Developer performs DOM changes | Developer describes desired UI
Manual synchronization         | State drives rendering
Manual list rebuilding         | JSX maps data into elements
DOM logic handled explicitly   | React manages DOM commits


key={todo.id} helps React identify list items between renders.

